vimrc_set.txt is what /opt/homebrew/bin/vim prints for ":set NAME?" after
reading the real ~/.vimrc, one row per option: the name, a tab, and the line
vim printed, leading white space and all.

Regenerated by TestTheVimrcsOptionsArePrintedTheWayVimPrintsThem when vim and
the vimrc are both on the machine -- the test re-runs vim and fails if this
file has drifted from it, so a brew upgrade that changes a default turns into
a named failure rather than a gate that passes against a stale answer.

The vim run is "--clean" plus ":source ~/.vimrc", not "-u ~/.vimrc", because
"-u file" skips defaults.vim and pvim's built-in defaults are defaults.vim's.
The one option the two disagree about is 'nrformats': bin,hex with
defaults.vim and bin,octal,hex without it.

'compatible' has no row. vim answers "nocompatible"; pvim takes the option and
keeps no value for it, so ":set compatible?" is E518 there. pvim is never
compatible and nothing reads the flag, so the row would be measuring a field
that does not exist.
